  {
    "episode_id": 72630,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72630.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "So, Deborah, thank you very much and thank you for addressing this so very, very important issue for Northwest Indiana. At the backbone of Northwest Indiana, as I always mentioned is our steel industry and the United Steel Workers. It's also our building trades. It's also our construction trades. It's the iron workers it's electricians. It's many unions. It's the teachers unions. It's the postal workers. It's across the board. Unionization is something that is very important to working men and women across the first congressional district. So my background is first and foremost, my grandfather was a steel worker. He was in local 1010. He worked there for 55 years, came over from Czechoslovakia. My father, who was born and raised in the Chicago and became an Indiana State Senator, had a very high ranking and has a very high voting record for the unions and the AFL-CIO, and has consistently been a champion for working men and women. My predecessor, Congressman Viscowski, has set the bar at a very high level of being a champion and advocate for working men and women. and union labor throughout Northwest Indiana. There wasn't a union hall that you would walk into that you didn't hear people yelling Pete when he would walk in. And that was a sign of respect for him because he was one of them. And so we work every single day as a staff and as a member of Congress to make sure that we're protecting men and women who are in labor unions. In my past electoral experience, I was a township trustee that when there would be work stoppages or there would be strikes, we were on the at the picket lines, we were in the union halls making sure that you would have a safety net for those individuals who would run out of resources while that was going on or who were out of a job because of the global market or dumping from foreign countries. And so near and dear to my heart is making sure that we protect unions every single day. So this pro act, is something that we passed in the 117th Congress. It did pass last year. And so now it is on the Senate side waiting for a ratification or for it to be brought up as far as legislation. And I'm sure towards the end of the conversation, you may ask, well, what can we do to see to make sure that this passes and it's to contact the United States senators via email or phone call letting them know that you support this. And so the PRO Act is really something that is vital because it gives unions the right to collectively bargain. It gives them for a fair election. It also allows workers to stand together to form a union and to have the ability to use their collective voices for fair wages. Also what we always have to consider is safe work environments, trade unions and all unions put an emphasis on safety and making sure not only are things built on time on schedule but safely and that everyone who goes to a job returns home from that job. And so union labor also, something that I believe is near and dear to my heart. So I'm a freshman member of Congress, I get sworn in on January 3rd, I take an oath On January 6th, we have events happen the insurrection or the riots at the capitol. I only bring that up because consistently what I have been saying, and I mean this wholeheartedly, is that what I am here to do is to close that gap, chip away at that divide. So what was very, very clear was that there's a divide in our nation. And very often workers think that they're being left behind or feel that they're being left behind. And if we could focus on the middle class, if we can focus on workers when we talk about renewable energies and new economies, when we talk about American jobs plan or an infrastructure bill, if we're bringing American workers along and we have a by American provision, and we focus on the domestic steel and manufacturing. of what is going on in making sure that when we rebuild our roads, bridges, waterways, dams, water pipelines, infrastructure, our electricity grid that we bring workers along. If we do that, I believe that will chip away at the divide. So the PRO Act has a lot to do with giving unions the right to collectively bargain, to have fair elections, to put themselves in a position to regain ground that may have been taken away from unions' bargaining rights. over the last decade or so. You had mentioned Indiana and one of the first states to pass the right to work. So some might say, well, who might that impact? If you can go back in history, and I was there on the Indiana State Capitol stairs, there were over 15,000 men and women who showed up to protest the Right to Work Act, 15,000. The entire state capital was full of people on the outside and on the lawn and inside. And so we must remember that the middle class and working men and women and having the ability to have a fair wage is what's at stake. And so I'd be happy to have this discussion and dive into it, but that's a little bit of my perspective of where we come from. And as a member of Congress, how I view our labor unions in northwest Indiana.",
