    "turn_text": "That's great. And I do want to say that I think There are many ways in addition to the right to work laws that were passed in the various states. There were lots of ways that at least our state administration was able to really chip away at unions. They made it more difficult to collect union dues. I think they just passed a law or considered a law that didn't required like first teacher unions require the school corporation to get them to get teachers to opt in every so often, which is was another hurdle to collect dues and other ways and you know many many businesses that used to be unions. Shops are not union shops anymore and it has of course driven down wages driven down benefits. And so that is why this is so important now. And I'd like to add a little bit, too, to the, you know, the number of unions that you mentioned. I always think that at this time that the service employees union is really important. These are the really low paid workers that really need a union more than a lot of industries. You know, so it's caregivers for the elderly, childcare workers, you know, mostly women, you know, in service industry jobs and wouldn't it be something? I know that we're talking about calling childcare infrastructure, but this would be another avenue for women in the workforce to, you know, get childcare so that they could get to work. And so I think that's a really important thing to remember too, that why this is so important, that this is a way for particularly women to overcome a lot of the hurdles that they have to fully participate in getting paid the same wages in whatever industry. So I know that the PRO Act, it's empowers the National Labor Relations Board a little more to enforce bad behavior by employers. And it does do away with the right to work laws, which is excellent, so that anyone who's working at a union shop and is benefiting from union services is required to pay into the union. I mean, that makes it fair in my mind. Another aspect of it is that, and we've seen a lot lately, this is another way that we've tipped away at unions is to call people contractors instead of employees. And so I think this pro act does something to address that, makes it harder for employers to call what we would consider employees independent contractors, which keeps them from getting any benefits as an employee. So, tell us what else is in this pro act and why this is going to help us.",
