    "turn_text": "Well, you had mentioned the National Labor Relations Board and what that focuses on under the current law. It's unable to enforce its own orders. When an entity does not comply with the Board's orders, the National Labor Relations Board must seek enforcement through the U.S. Courts of Appeals. The bill would authorize the National Labor Relations Board to enforce its own orders requiring employers that failed to comply with the labor board to pay a civil monetary penalty up to $10,000 for each violation. And so what that does is it circumfence where people were going around the National Labor Relations Board in order to provide, instead of going to the courts, it has a direct violation. And so you had also mentioned the focus on the right to work and how that impacts. The right to work and ultimately I kind of want to go in a couple different areas, but I want to you know How the how we focused in Congress You know, we always have to keep in perspective COVID-19 and what we were under over the last year and a half. And there was a great deal of uncertainty in the workplace. A lot of the proactive had a lot to do with making sure OSHA was protecting workers because there were many essential workers were union labor that were out there on the roads and the bridges and the mills in the supermarkets in the classrooms. And so the PRO Act actually made sure that there was safe working conditions and that we were addressing COVID-19. And as a matter of priorities, we passed an apprenticeship program, early on in the legislative cycle in January, February, making sure that we put programming and resources towards apprenticeship programs so that we have a workforce that is developed",
