    "turn_text": "to go. throughout the United States and throughout North, Northwest Indiana, the first congressional district, putting our resources in there, making sure that it's accessible to women, making sure that it's accessible to minorities, making sure that it's accessible to those who want a job can prepare to be in the trades. I bring that up because the next thing we did is we passed the PROAC, making sure that we're protecting the middle class. And as we protect the middle class and we get them to work and they have the right to collectively bargain, they have the right to earn a fair wage, they have a right to safe working conditions, they have the right to a pension that is promised to them that they had put in for. So those things are extremely important because it was a matter of priority to make sure that we were doing that and immediately it was only a day apart we passed the rescue plan making sure that we were getting people a national strategic plan for people to get shots to be vaccinated making sure that dollars were spent on infrastructure and sent to the communities the school systems so that we could get kids back in school safely and making sure that we held up the economy. And so those are the things that were extremely important to us as a Congress and going forward now, now that we have the pro act and in passing the house, we have to make this law. We have to make sure that we have deliverables. So passing the United States Senate is extremely important, and having the Biden and Harris administration sign this into law is vital. And there has been no better friend to labor than the Biden and Harris administration. And what I want to see, make sure, as you mentioned, we passed it in the 107th Congress. Now we've passed it in the 117th, and the pro act is vital. And so we have to have your voices be heard if you're pro union and if you're a union member to the United States Senate. And so I kind of wanted to touch base on those things and be happy to answer any other questions you may have.",
