  {
    "episode_id": 72630,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72630.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Well, and that's great. And I think, you know, I hope people will understand that what the PRO Act allows is for, you know, workers, because people have forgotten. They've forgotten what unions did for workers. And, you know, it's not just, you know, higher wages, it's better benefits. Nobody can afford insurance anymore. You know, so it's, you know, it's better benefits. It's, you know, better health care. And, you know, it's better support for families. And I hope that workers will see this act as an opportunity to come together and really make their lives better after so many years of unions just taking a beating every year, it seems like. And of course, one of the worst last things was during the last presidential administration telling the meatpacking industry that nobody has to oversee your system. You can just do that yourself. And I frankly stopped eating pork at that point. The notion that no longer would anybody be overseeing any meat processing that was independent of a paycheck from the person who wanted to keep the line going really was kind of one of the things that threw me over. But again, this is something that unions can address these kinds of safety issues in the workplace. And even, you know, in Indiana for two years, we tried to pass a pregnancy accommodation bill that would allow pregnant women in employment to, you know, get maybe a five minute more break, you know, or to, you know, have more chances to go to the restroom. And we couldn't get that passed in Indiana. All we passed was a ridiculous bill that says you can ask your employer, but they don't have to respond.",
